// A farm that declares a food service and grows nothing at all: no
// `output_per_tick` and no `max_stock`. It is the same error as
// `unsustainable_capacity.ron` taken to its limit — a capacity of 20 residents
// against an output that sustains none — and for a long time it was the one
// shape neither crate refused. `validate_production` says nothing, because a
// building with no output and no stock is also exactly what a well and a house
// look like and it cannot see the service; `check_food_capacity` skipped it,
// because it read the two fields as a pair and gave up when either was missing.
//
// Declaring the role did not make this one go away, and that is worth knowing:
// a provider that grows nothing is still a perfectly well-formed provider. What
// the sum type removed is the *other* half — an output with no granary, or a
// granary with no output — and this fixture is the half that survives.
//
// What the game did with it is the reason this fixture exists: the coverage
// never consults the production, so the phantom farm won its houses on
// distance, they were served for ever and fed never, and their food
// satisfaction climbed to the ceiling on an empty stock. Absorbing hunger, the
// state this whole check exists to make unreachable.
//
// The house and the well come **after** the farm, so the indices in the
// expected report do not move. They are here for the reason spelled out in
// `unsustainable_capacity.ron`: without them the cross-table checks fill the
// report with a missing house and a missing water provider, and bury the one
// error this fixture is about.
(
    buildings: [
        (
            id: "phantom_farm",
            size: (2, 2),
            cost: 40,
            levels: 1,
            role: "provider",
            service: Some((kind: "food", range_per_level: [10], capacity_per_level: [20])),
            required_services: [],
        ),
        (
            id: "house",
            size: (1, 1),
            cost: 10,
            levels: 3,
            role: "house",
            service: None,
            required_services: ["water", "food"],
        ),
        (
            id: "well",
            size: (1, 1),
            cost: 12,
            levels: 1,
            role: "provider",
            service: Some((kind: "water", range_per_level: [12], capacity_per_level: [32])),
            required_services: [],
        ),
    ],
)
